<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <!--
    The hardware end of the audio path. Exists as its own project for one reason: Core must
    never reference NAudio, and a project reference is the only way to say that in a way the
    build enforces (architecture.md §3). Everything above IAudioSink is queue policy and lives
    in Core, where it is testable with no audio device present.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\D47.Core\D47.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The sample providers are internal because nothing outside this assembly should be building
    NAudio graph nodes, and they are also the part that can be wrong with no sound card involved
    — PCM widening, the loop point, and the render tap's running sample position. So the tests
    reach them directly, the same arrangement D47.Tts makes for its wire format.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="D47.App.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="D47.Audio.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NAudio" Version="2.2.1" />

    <!--
      WebRTC AEC3, which is what architecture.md §2 names for echo cancellation. The wrapper is
      MIT and the native webrtc-apm it carries is BSD-3, so the licence constraint in §1 holds
      through the graph rather than only at the top of it.

      It pulls SoundFlow along as a transitive dependency and none of it is used: NAudio still
      owns capture and render, and what is taken from this package is the four types around the
      audio processing module. Weighed against the alternative — hand-writing an adaptive filter
      and a delay estimator, then discovering what a speaker's nonlinearity and two clocks do to
      it — a megabyte of unused engine is the cheap side of the trade.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="SoundFlow.Extensions.WebRtc.Apm" Version="1.4.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
